course: dictionary
lesson: 8519
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words and sentences around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Pass the tomato sauce, please.» Here the words tomato and pass show that sauce is food.
explain: «The Mole shouted «Onion-sauce!» at the rabbits as a cheeky jeer.» The rabbits are not being served a dish, so this sauce is not food. Onion sauce was served with roast rabbit, so the joke is rude. Sauce here means cheeky talk.
explain: Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try your meaning in place of the word. If it makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning. The context tells you which one is used.
fact: «Sauce» means a thick liquid served with food to add flavour, such as tomato sauce, gravy or chocolate sauce.
fact: In British slang, «sauce» means cheeky talk, a rude or teasing remark.
fact: Ketchup is a tomato sauce. Many experts think its name comes from a Chinese fish sauce, but this is still argued about.
fact: Clues like food words, feasts or jokes can tell you which meaning of a word the writer means.
exercise: «Pass the tomato sauce, please.» What does sauce mean here? | *A thick liquid served with food to add flavour | Cheeky talk | A slice of bread | A cooking pan
exercise: Your little brother says, «Less of your sauce!» after you tease him. What does sauce mean here? | *Cheeky talk | A kind of bread | A bottle top | A thick liquid for dinner
exercise: Which of these is a sauce? | *Gravy | Porridge | Olives | Bread
test: «Dip your chips in the sauce.» What is the sauce? | A type of cheese | Cheeky talk | A bottle of water | *A thick liquid food for dipping
test: The rabbits laughed at «Onion-sauce!» because it was a rude joke. What does sauce mean here? | A cook's tool | *Cheeky talk | A thick liquid for dinner | A tomato
exam: Ketchup, cheese sauce and soy sauce are all examples of what? | Types of bread | Cooking pans | *Thick liquids served with food to add flavour | Cheeky jokes
